   "text": "The four jobs of a manager. Here is the idea in one sentence: being a manager is not a title, it is four jobs you do every week. Job one: get the work done. Decide what matters, tell people what is expected, check work before it is due, and fix problems at the cause. Job two: take care of your people. Listen and help, grow them, thank them for specific work, and trust them with real decisions. Job three: make things better. Explain the why behind changes, describe where the team is going, let people try new ideas, and look back after big work. Job four: connect your team to the rest of Vanderbilt. Know the people your team depends on, watch for what is coming, and speak up for your team with facts. Every manager does all four. Most weeks need one of them more than the others, and part of the job is noticing which. These four jobs come from research by Gary Yukl. Vanderbilt uses them as a guide, and the survey you will receive asks about the same habits. Now tap each card on the page to see the habits inside each job."
  },
