   "narration": "https://dnznrvs05pmza.cloudfront.net/text_to_speech/69c60dd0-f128-41e2-9cf4-d62e69a10fc2/MVF5_video_external.mp3?_jwt=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJrZXlIYXNoIjoiZmI2ODE5MDg4ZTlkMzhiYiIsImJ1Y2tldCI6InJ1bndheS10YXNrLWFydGlmYWN0cyIsInN0YWdlIjoicHJvZCIsImV4cCI6MTc4OTE5OTM2OH0.hlFmOQddmHiUlUEUusj69KnT9Wjcu32Ikl5GdFT0jnc",
   "text": "Job four: connect your team to the rest of Vanderbilt. Why it matters: your team needs things it does not control: approvals, information, help from other offices, and a fair hearing when someone asks too much of you. Most new managers do not know this is part of the job, so it is the one they do least. Three habits. Know the people: your HR partner, your Engagement Consultant, the person in Finance who approves your requests, and a peer manager in another unit, by name, before you need any of them. Watch for what is coming: read the compensation calendar in August and tell your team in September what October will bring, instead of hearing it from them. Speak up for your team: when another office’s request would break your month, show the hours, name the conflict, offer two options, and negotiate the date, instead of absorbing it or refusing it. What to stop doing: meeting your HR partner at the first crisis. This week: have coffee with one person your team depends on. Now flip the three cards, then turn the page and make the call on that request."
